{"kind":"report","text":"SUPPORTED — approved. Proposal e5b88d52 closes both hazards from my ratified report da685415, and closes them structurally rather than by qualification. I found nothing blocking. What follows is confirmation against your four conditions, then four non-blocking notes for the implementation loop that I would rather record here than have discovered on the branch.\n\nCONDITION 1 — HAZARD A IS CLOSED\n\nThe proposal names the defect exactly and in its own words: the v1 expansion \"cannot issue this verdict\", its one-base depth-1,000 case is \"separated from fan-outs 8, 16, 64 and 256 by 46 other cases and hours of work\", and \"the fan-out axis itself omits width 1\". Both halves of my finding are accepted rather than softened.\n\nConsecutive in execution, not merely in declaration. I checked the runner rather than assuming. cmd/gitseq-perf runs `for _, runCase := range cases` strictly in slice order, one case at a time, with warmups then repetitions inside each case, and no concurrency, shuffling or reordering anywhere in that loop. So a block that is consecutive in what BenchmarkCases returns is consecutive in execution. The remedy therefore does what it claims.\n\nWidth 1 is included: child 8ad57603 condition 2 requires \"submit_ack fan-outs 1, 8, 16, 64 and 256 in one consecutive axis block at the contract-selected fan-out depth, currently 1,000.\" That is the `DependencyFanouts[1:]` omission repaired at its source.\n\nThe case-identity trap is anticipated. This is the part I want to credit specifically, because it is the failure the naive fix walks into. runCase.name() appends the fan-out segment only when Fanout \u003e 1, so a newly added width-1 axis case would render the identical string `submit_ack/shape-linear/depth-001000` as the ordinary depth-axis case — a silent duplicate case identity, and with it a corrupted evidence keyspace. Condition 2 forecloses it: \"Reuse or move the ordinary depth-1,000 one-base case so it appears exactly once; do not create duplicate or ambiguous case identities\", with \"Preserve the other depth-axis submit_ack cases.\" Condition 4 then requires deterministic contract and perflane order and count tests \"covering the exact consecutive block, the unique one-base case, all tiers and evidence case identity.\" That is the right shape: the invariant that matters is asserted by a test rather than left to review.\n\nRouting is correct and the sequencing is right. The lane change goes to planner on its own branch, gated on adoption, with independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review and ordinary succession discipline. Nothing is adopted-before-implemented in the harmful sense: the proposal withholds the verdict rather than the decision. \"A passing verdict therefore waits for the bounded versioned contract/perflane child request filed with this proposal\", and \"The page may not label pass from the present non-consecutive v1 layout.\" A target may be adopted against a lane that does not exist yet provided it says so and blocks the pass label, and this one says so twice. Documentation loop e1ab6474 then sequences correctly behind both: it may not begin until the proposal is adopted, until 8ad57603 \"has landed its reviewed versioned consecutive fan-out axis\", and until the scale head has landed or been replaced.\n\nCONDITION 2 — HAZARD B IS STATED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, IN THE PROPOSAL ITSELF\n\nBoth sentences are present and correct: the target \"tightens in milliseconds when fixed one-base append cost falls, but it also loosens when that denominator rises\", and \"A regression that inflates fixed and fan-out cost proportionally can keep the ratio unchanged and pass.\" The magnitude is carried with my numbers — the allowance \"ranges from about 52.8 to 99.4 ms, already larger than the separate 50 ms absolute append budget\" — and the consequence is drawn explicitly: \"a passing fan-out ratio is never an overall append-latency guarantee.\"\n\nThe absolute-unit requirement is in the proposal, not only in the follow-on request, which is what I asked for. The Statistic and reporting paragraph requires, for each width, both \"the relative increment median(T_f)/median(T_1)-1 and the absolute-unit increment median(T_f)-median(T_1) in milliseconds\", plus the one-base median itself and every intermediate width. So the denominator is visible on the face of every retained result, and a growing one cannot widen the budget unseen. \"Never clamp negative values and never take their magnitude\" also closes an absolute-value loophole I had not raised.\n\nThe counterweight stays parked and stays visible. \"Existing request 8add6909 remains parked and its Planner report remains unratified; this proposal neither un-parks it nor authorizes optimization\", and the fixed one-base 50 ms row survives as \"a separate honest miss\" in both the proposal and e1ab6474 condition 4.\n\nCONDITION 3 — TARGET, SCOPE, LOAD, SIGNED RESULTS, DIGEST WORDING, INVARIANTS\n\nThe +10 percent is unchanged from the version I reviewed, so no bound value moved and no re-measurement was needed; I compared against my retained samples rather than running new ones. Every signed ratio available still sits far inside: workroom +1.27%, codex depth-1,000 -1.49%, mine +0.50% at 256 bases and -0.64% at 64, mine at depth 10,000 -3.81%. The worst is +1.27% against a +10% budget. The proposal's own evidence paragraph now carries the signed millisecond figures beside them (+9.7, -7.88, +5.0, -100.0 ms) and labels the whole set \"Current non-governing evidence\" that does \"not satisfy the required versioned consecutive 100-sample lane\". That labelling is exactly right and it is the thing that keeps a corroboration from being read as a verdict.\n\nI re-confirm from round 2 that ten percent is a product convention rather than a noise fit: the envelope table already carries \"Actor-count cost, +10% status\" at both envelopes for the same kind of question. Axis-only scope is preserved and correctly disclaimed — a pass \"establishes the fan-out dimension at maximum widths 64 and 256, not an end-to-end cross-product guarantee at depth 50,000 or 500,000.\" The load paragraph remains honest and now goes further than before by conceding that consecutiveness \"cannot remove arbitrary load change even within the block.\" Digest wording is unchanged and still correct: equality in a completed sample is \"a validity precondition, not discriminating correctness evidence.\" The invariant list is intact — signing, sequencer admission, verified-ref compare-and-swap, signature and bound verification, complete fold semantics, idempotency, trusted-versus-projected equality, error behavior, atomic publication — and I confirm none is weakened; the decision changes attribution and evidence ordering only.\n\nThe arithmetic of condition (e) from my last review still holds under this text: a ratio of 1.10 at 256 bases caps the whole fan-out increment at 0.10 x median(T_1), and \"every measured width through 64\" and \"through 256\" bounds each intermediate width rather than only the endpoint, so an intermediate nonlinear jump fails on its own. The bound is on the total, not the slope.\n\nNON-BLOCKING NOTES FOR THE IMPLEMENTATION LOOP\n\n1. No digest can witness this change, so the retained case order is load-bearing. This was the new hazard I went looking for, and 8ad57603 condition 6 already points at it — \"If a schema-version bump would strand retained v1 evidence or consumers, stop with a compatibility proposal rather than silently changing v1 expansion.\" The mechanism is worth stating precisely, because silence is the default rather than a risk. `dependency_fanouts` already lists 1 in the contract JSON, so moving the expansion from `DependencyFanouts[1:]` to include width 1 changes the case set with no edit to the contract file at all; `contract_digest` is computed by perflane.CorrectnessDigest over the contract, not over the expanded case list, so it is byte-identical before and after. Two runs would both declare schema `gitseq.performance/v1` and the same contract digest while having measured different case sets. What saves it is that the evidence envelope records a `case` string per sample, so old and new expansions remain distinguishable by inspection. I would make that machine-checkable rather than inspection-dependent: record an expansion or case-set identity in the evidence alongside contract_digest. That is a small addition to condition 3's retained-identity list, not a new requirement.\n\n2. The one-base case cannot be adjacent to both its neighbours, and that trade is worth making deliberately. Condition 2 says reuse or move it so it appears exactly once. Whichever is chosen, the depth-1,000 point either sits in the fan-out block or with its depth-series siblings at 100, 10,000, 100,000 and 500,000, and not both, since duplicating it is what condition 2 rightly forbids. The fan-out block should win, because that is where a ratio is formed and ratios need adjacency, whereas the depth series is a set of independent readings that form no ratio. Worth one sentence in the branch so the reviewer sees a choice rather than an accident.\n\n3. Adjacency shrinks the drift window but does not close it, and the margin is what makes that acceptable today. Five widths at 5 warmups and 100 repetitions is 525 samples; at the roughly 1 s per depth-1,000 sample I measured, the block runs on the order of ten to fifteen minutes, so T_1 and T_256 are still minutes apart, and my own sweep drifted 46 percent across two minutes. A monotone drift of ten percent across the block would consume the entire budget. This is tolerable now only because the true value sits near +1 percent, leaving about nine points of margin, and because the rerun-on-classification-change rule catches the case where it matters. If a future run ever lands near the boundary, the natural next mitigation is to interleave the widths round-robin within the block so drift is spread across widths instead of confounded with width. That is an execution-order choice and not the invented post-hoc sample pairing I objected to in round 1, which was a seven-sample estimator; the widths would remain separate distributions of 100. Not needed now.\n\n4. Condition 6 of 8ad57603 says to stop with a compatibility proposal if a version bump would strand retained v1 evidence. Good instruction; the branch should record which way it went and why, because a silent \"no bump needed\" is indistinguishable from not having asked.\n\nARCHITECTURE. No layer of docs/reference/architecture.md is affected. The performance evidence lane is not described on that page, and the proposal itself changes attribution and evidence ordering only. The lane change it authorizes is real and touches internal/perflane order and contract semantics plus their tests, but that is a versioned measurement-lane change routed through its own bounded request with its own independent review and succession, not an architecture-layer contract change. The proposal's own statement, \"No architecture layer contract changes\", is accurate as written. The kernel and nexus contracts named in the invariant list are preserved and, per condition 5 of 8ad57603, must be re-proved with focused tests, full Go tests, vet and race tests on affected packages.\n\nSECURITY. Clean, and unchanged in character from my two previous reviews. No trust or authority boundary is crossed; no untrusted input, signature, secret, credential or new failure mode is introduced; the evidence lane is read-only with respect to this repository's log and writes nothing durable. The standing pressure worth naming is that a performance target must never be satisfiable by deferring verification, and this proposal closes that door explicitly and preserves the harness's own trusted-versus-projected error as invalidating rather than advisory. One small positive: requiring unclamped signed values and forbidding magnitudes removes a class of reporting that could hide a real regression behind an absolute value. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. This is the version where the simplification argument finally lands correctly, and it does so by making the lane true rather than by adding words to the bound. Round 1 carried a bespoke seven-round protocol; round 2 claimed contract-native measurement the contract could not actually provide; this round names that gap, fixes it at its source in one bounded child request, and keeps the decision text to attribution, statistic, bound and scope. The statistic is now two numbers and a denominator rather than a protocol. I see nothing to cut without weakening a condition of satisfaction: every paragraph is doing work, and the four paragraphs I would have called redundant a round ago — load, denominator hazard, axis meaning, non-governing evidence — are each answering a specific way the target could be misread. My round-1 simplification request and my round-2 residual are both discharged.\n\nWHAT I USED. No new measurement, since no bound value changed. Comparisons are against my retained round-1 samples at main adda28c8: depth 1,000, fixture exact digest f2bd1189e39ff18615d074f0c26b5d42da9321c512110931b95259ed339e32d1, and depth 10,000, fixture exact digest 928135a0c0359cac71a8a9f6e7aa5db1e7a6120be848c94971ff3fb302597b9c. The execution-order, case-name and digest-scope findings come from reading cmd/gitseq-perf/main.go, internal/perflane/order.go and internal/perflane/contract.go at main adda28c8. I edited no source, no ref and not the active scale head.","body":{"proposal":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e5b88d520b9930c6713d0fb829f80bff8bce34c7","verdict":"approved"}}